<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the generated Info.plist by the Tauri CLI. Only keys the CLI does
  not already produce belong here — do not restate the name, version or bundle
  identifier, which come from tauri.conf.json.

  These are the TCC purpose strings. macOS shows the matching one in the consent
  dialog the first time a scan reaches a protected folder, and an app that has
  no string for a folder it touches gets a generic prompt or is refused outright.

  They are written to say what Swept actually does with the access, and to be
  true about the limits, because this dialog is the only place many users will
  read anything about the app's intentions. "To improve your experience" would
  be worthless here.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Swept lists the largest and oldest files on your Desktop so you can see what is taking up space. It never removes anything from here without you selecting it first.</string>

	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Swept lists the largest and oldest files in your Documents folder so you can see what is taking up space. It never removes anything from here without you selecting it first.</string>

	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Swept lists the largest and oldest files in your Downloads folder so you can see what is taking up space. It never removes anything from here without you selecting it first.</string>

	<key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Swept measures space used on connected volumes so its totals are accurate. It does not clean removable volumes.</string>

	<!--
	  Swept asks the Finder to move files to the Trash, so macOS shows an
	  automation consent prompt naming Swept the first time. Without this string
	  that prompt has no explanation in it.

	  This is not obvious from the code, which is why it is written down: the
	  `trash` crate's macOS default is DeleteMethod::Finder, which shells out to
	  osascript -- not NSFileManager. See entitlements.plist for why no
	  apple-events entitlement is needed despite that.
	-->
	<key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Swept asks the Finder to move the items you confirmed to the Trash, so that they stay recoverable and can be put back. It is used for nothing else.</string>

	<!--
	  Declares that the app uses no non-exempt encryption, which is true: it has
	  no network code at all. Without this key, every notarized upload asks the
	  export-compliance question again. Revisit if the updater (roadmap D3) ever
	  ships, since TLS is exempt but the answer should be made deliberately.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	  Swept reads and writes only inside the user's home directory and offers no
	  document types, so it opts out of being a document-based app entirely.
	-->
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.utilities</string>

	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>Copyright © 2026 cha-ndler. Provided as is, with no warranty. See TERMS.md.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
